🟢 What are Docker Image Layers?
👉 Docker image is made of multiple layers stacked together
From bottom to top:
Base Layer
OS (like Linux)

Layer 1
Installed packages

Layer 2
Application dependencies

Container Layer (Top)
Writable layer when container runs

👉 All layers are read-only
👉 Only top container layer is writable

⚙️ How it works
When you run a container:
Docker adds a new writable layer
All changes (files, logs, updates) happen here
👉 Original image layers remain unchanged

🔥 Why Layers are Useful
Faster builds (reuse layers)
Less storage (shared layers)
Easy updates

🎯 Example
If 2 containers use same image:
Base + layers are shared
Only top layer is different

#  Docker — Port Binding
Port binding means:
👉 Connecting a container’s port to your system (host) port

⚙️ Syntax
docker run -p HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT IMAGE_NAME
🧠 Example
docker run -p 8080:80 nginx
8080 → Host (your system)
80 → Container (inside Docker)

👉 Access in browser:
http://localhost:8080

🎯 Why Port Binding is Needed
Containers are isolated
Without port binding → app is not accessible outside
It allows external users to connect
🔥 Key Points
Host port can be anything
Container port is fixed (depends on app)
One host port → one service

